<h2>Everything a café counter needs</h2>
<p>A café sells with its eyes as much as its coffee. The counter has to show sandwiches, cakes and pastries at their best, keep them at a safe temperature through the day and leave room for the coffee machine and the till. This collection brings together the equipment cafés, coffee shops, sandwich bars and bakery cafés buy most, from the front counter to the kitchen behind it.</p>

<h2>At the counter: displays that sell</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Grab and go fridges</strong> – open-fronted displays around 1.45–1.5 m high for packaged sandwiches, salads, yoghurts and drinks. Placed by the till, they turn the queue into impulse sales. Standard models run +5 to +7 °C; the premium mirrored stainless steel models run −1 to +7 °C for dairy and cooked meats.</li>
<li><strong>Patisserie and cake fridges</strong> – closed glass cabinets that protect cakes and desserts. Slimline double-glazed models start at around £698 + VAT; premium double-glazed models with curved or flat glass suit high-end coffee shops.</li>
<li><strong>Counter and countertop displays</strong> – double-glazed chilled displays that form the serving counter or sit on top of an existing one, at 0 to +5 °C.</li>
<li><strong>Heated pastry displays</strong> – keep croissants, pasties and toasted products hot (+65 to +90 °C) and visible, served from the back through sliding doors.</li>
<li><strong>Upright cake towers</strong> – glass on four sides, a centrepiece for cafés with limited counter space.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Behind the counter</h2>
<p>An under counter fridge (about 125 litres in a 600 mm footprint) keeps milk and dairy alternatives within reach of the barista, so the main fridge stays closed during the rush. For bake-off and toasties, our compact stainless steel ovens and the oven and grill with steamer run from a standard 13-amp socket, and the cast iron contact grill heats from 50 to 300 °C for paninis.</p>

<h2>Planning a café layout</h2>
<ul>
<li>Put the grab and go fridge where the queue forms and the patisserie display next to the till, so the decision is made before the order.</li>
<li>Keep displays away from the coffee machine’s steam and from sunny windows – both make glass mist and the refrigeration work harder.</li>
<li>Match heights and finishes: our standard grab and go and patisserie fridges share the same 1450 mm height and silver or black finishes, so they line up neatly.</li>
<li>Check the floor and the delivery route: a 1200 mm grab and go weighs about 240 kg.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Standard or coffee-chain specification?</h2>
<p>Independent cafés usually start with the standard silver or black ranges, which offer excellent value. Several premium models in mirrored 304 stainless steel – grab and go, closed patisserie and multidecks with castors – are built to the specification used by national coffee chains, for sites that are restocked several times a day and cleaned every night.</p>

<h2>Food safety in a café</h2>
<p>Keep chilled food at 8 °C or below (most cafés aim for 1–5 °C), hot food on display at 63 °C or above, and record the temperatures of every display and fridge each day. Label products with allergen information and rotate stock so the oldest date is always at the front.</p>
