<h2>Cold storage and display for delis and butchers</h2>
<p>Delis and butchers depend on their refrigeration more than almost any other shop: fresh meat, poultry, fish, cheese and cooked meats have to stay at the right temperature from delivery to counter, look appetising behind the glass, and the shop is inspected on it. This page brings together the cabinets delis, butchers, fishmongers and farm shops use – from storage at the back to the display at the front, with slimline options for shops where space is tight.</p>

<h2>Back of shop: storage</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stainless steel meat fridges</strong> – upright gastronorm chillers and a fish/meat cabinet holding −5 to +5 °C, colder than a standard fridge, with forced-air cooling for an even temperature. Single door models (about 740 mm wide, four shelves) and a double door model (about 1440 mm wide, eight shelves), all 900 mm deep for full-size trays.</li>
<li><strong>Meat freezers</strong> – matching stainless steel gastronorm freezers at −18 to −24 °C for frozen lines, burgers and sausages.</li>
<li><strong>Refrigerated prep counters</strong> – two and three door stainless steel counters at −5 to +5 °C, suitable for meat and designed for rooms up to 43 °C, with a worktop for cutting and packing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Front of shop: display</h2>
<p>A <strong>serve over counter</strong> is the classic butcher’s display: customers see the products through the front glass and staff serve, weigh and wrap from behind. Our slim serve overs come in 1000, 1200, 1500 and 2000 mm widths with chilled storage underneath, and can be combined into a longer counter. Set them at the colder end of their 0 to +12 °C range for meat and fish.</p>

<h2>For delis and farm shops</h2>
<p>A deli counter sells cheese, charcuterie, olives, salads and prepared dishes, usually at 0 to +5 °C. The slim serve overs give a deep display with storage underneath, and slimline grab and go fridges (about 1450 mm high, from 600 mm wide) present pre-packed portions, sandwiches and drinks for customers who don’t want to queue. Patisserie displays add cakes and desserts. Keep cheese and cooked meats apart from raw meat, ideally in a separate cabinet, and use separate utensils and boards.</p>

<h2>Hygiene and food safety</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep raw and ready-to-eat products in separate cabinets, or clearly separated with their own utensils, as your food safety plan requires.</li>
<li>Record cabinet temperatures at least daily and check products with a probe.</li>
<li>Choose stainless steel interiors and removable racks – they are the easiest to clean and sanitise.</li>
<li>Wipe spills immediately and clean door gaskets regularly to prevent odours.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Setting up a new deli or butcher’s shop</h2>
<p>A typical new shop starts with a double door meat fridge at the back, a gastronorm freezer, a two door prep counter and a 1500–2000 mm serve over at the front. Stainless steel gastronorm cabinets weigh about 140–200 kg, so plan the delivery route, and leave ventilation space around every cabinet.</p>
