	'display-freezers'   => array(
		'short'    => '<p>Display freezers for shops, cafés, canteens and supermarkets – upright glass door freezers and sliding glass lid chest freezers that keep frozen food and ice cream at −18 °C or colder while customers see the range.</p>',
		'long'     => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Selling frozen food in your shop</h2>
<p>Frozen ready meals, vegetables, desserts and ice cream are planned purchases for some customers and impulse buys for others. A display freezer lets them see the range without opening a storage cabinet, keeps products at −18 °C or colder and protects their quality. This page shows the display freezers retailers, cafés, canteens and forecourts use most; the full range, with filters by size and type, is in <a href="/product-category/display-freezer/">display freezers</a>.</p>

<h2>Which display freezer for which business?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Convenience stores and forecourts</strong> – a single door upright glass door freezer (about 600 mm wide) for ready meals and ice cream tubs, plus a chest freezer by the till for lollies and ice.</li>
<li><strong>Supermarkets and farm shops</strong> – double door and large double hinged glass door uprights for a wider frozen range at eye level.</li>
<li><strong>Cafés, kiosks and ice cream parlours</strong> – the sliding glass lid chest freezer (about 1510 mm wide, about 500 litres in five baskets) for single portions and desserts.</li>
<li><strong>Canteens</strong> – an upright glass door freezer for desserts and frozen snacks next to the service line.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Upright or chest?</h2>
<p>Upright display freezers use less floor space and put products at eye level, where they sell best; the high-spec models have self-closing doors and LED lighting. Chest freezers hold temperature very well because cold air stays in the chest when the lid opens, and the glass lids let customers browse from above. Many shops use both: uprights for the core range, a chest for impulse lines near the till.</p>

<h2>Looking after frozen stock</h2>
<ul>
<li>Store and display at −18 °C or colder and record the temperature daily.</li>
<li>Never load above the load line, and don’t block the air vents inside the cabinet.</li>
<li>Put deliveries away quickly so frozen products don’t start to thaw.</li>
<li>Defrost when ice builds up and keep door gaskets clean and intact.</li>
<li>Keep freezers out of direct sunlight and away from heaters and entrance doors.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Prices and delivery</h2>
<p>Our sliding glass lid chest freezer starts at about £595 + VAT and upright glass door display freezers at about £850 + VAT, rising to about £1,900 + VAT for a large double door model. Delivery to UK mainland addresses is free; deliveries above York, Leeds and Blackpool or outside the mainland carry an extra charge.</p>
